# GRCh38 fixed silencer learning-curve training subsets

These datasets are generated by `src/make_GRCh38_learning_curves.py` from the
native-distribution paired master pool in
`../training/train_100_{pos,neg}.{bed,fa}`. The script must be called with the
SVN input and output directories.

The fixed master excludes:

- the paired chr1 pool, used as validation; and
- the paired chr2 pool, used as the final test set.

Validation and test files remain in `../training/` and are not copied or
percentage-subsetted here. Negative membership follows manifest source-pool
provenance rather than the genomic coordinate chosen for the synthetic null
sequence by `genNullSeqs()`.

## Nested-subset strategy

Positive and negative BED/FASTA pairs are shuffled independently exactly once
using deterministic random streams derived from seed 42. Every percentage is
an exact prefix of that fixed order:

```text
10% subset of 20% subset of 40% subset of 60% subset of 80% subset of 100%
```

Percentages use `floor(N * percentage / 100)`, with 100% set exactly to `N`.
For the 158,088-record-per-class SVN master training pool:

| percentage | positive records | negative records |
|-----------:|-----------------:|-----------------:|
| 10%        |           15,808 |           15,808 |
| 20%        |           31,617 |           31,617 |
| 40%        |           63,235 |           63,235 |
| 60%        |           94,852 |           94,852 |
| 80%        |          126,470 |          126,470 |
| 100%       |          158,088 |          158,088 |

## Files

For each percentage `P`, the learning-curve stage writes:

```text
train_P_pos.bed
train_P_pos.fa
train_P_neg.bed
train_P_neg.fa
```

BED and FASTA records remain synchronized by position. Sequence identifiers and
contents are preserved. `learning_curve_summary.json` records the seed,
rounding rule, record counts, and chromosome counts for every class and
percentage.

The builder validates written counts, unique FASTA identifiers, BED/FASTA
record counts, monotonic sizes, and exact prefix nesting. Each percentage must
train a newly initialized model; larger percentages must not continue training
from smaller-percentage models.
